          "\"Magari\" has three faces. With the indicative it means maybe: \"Magari passo domani\" is ‘Maybe I'll drop by tomorrow’. With the imperfect or pluperfect subjunctive it means if only: \"Magari fosse vero!\" is ‘If only it were true!’ And alone, as an answer, it means I wish: \"Vinci tu?\" \"Magari!\"",
          "\"Pure\" means too or even (\"Vengo pure io\"), but after an imperative it means go ahead: \"Entri pure\" is ‘Do come in’. \"Pur\" with a gerund means although: \"Pur essendo stanco, uscì.\" \"Anzi\" corrects or strengthens what came before: \"Non mi dispiace, anzi!\" is ‘I don't mind, quite the opposite!’ and \"domani, anzi dopodomani\" is ‘tomorrow, or rather the day after’.",
